- op: discuss  # e5da
  at: '2026-03-11T17:28:18Z'  # e5da
  by: agent  # e5da
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e5da
  clock: 9  # e5da
  nonce: e5da  # e5da
  message: "**Impact assessment of the \"cross-file disambiguation without type info\" gap:**\n\n**What the gap is:** When a C++ file calls `obj.Initialize()` and the definition of `Initialize()` is in a different file from where `obj` is declared, we can't always determine which class's `Initialize()` is being called. Our mitigations (1/sqrt(N) confidence scaling, same-class preference, inline method qualification) handle most cases, but cross-file calls to common method names remain ambiguous.\n\n**Impact: LOW to MODERATE.** Here's why:\n\n1. **The mitigations already handle the high-impact cases.** The 1/sqrt(N) confidence scaling means that `Initialize()` with 15 candidate classes gets confidence 0.18, which is below the default edge filtering threshold (0.5). These false edges are effectively invisible in slices and rankings.\n\n2. **Same-file calls are correctly resolved.** The same-class preference and path_hint matching handle the most common case: calling methods on objects defined in the same file or the corresponding header.\n\n3. **True type-aware resolution requires a C++ type checker.** This means parsing #include chains, resolving templates, tracking variable declarations through control flow — essentially reimplementing a significant portion of a C++ compiler frontend. The effort-to-benefit ratio is very unfavorable.\n\n4. **Scope of affected repos:** In the bakeoff corpus, only 2-3 C++ repos (shaka-packager, firecracker's C components) showed significant false-positive edges from this gap. Most C++ codebases use reasonably distinctive method names or have methods defined in the same translation unit as their callers.\n\n**Recommendation:** Keep at HOLDING. The confidence-scaling mitigation is sufficient for practical use. Full type-aware resolution would be a multi-week effort with diminishing returns, better addressed if/when we integrate with clangd or a compile_commands.json-based approach."  # e5da
